<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 846 786" role="img" aria-labelledby="title desc">
<title id="title">Zane Wang — building tools that make AI coding agents more reliable</title>
<desc id="desc">A pixel-art sunset over a city drawn from the past 12 months of Zane's GitHub contributions. As the image loads, the contribution graph appears along the horizon and rises into the city: one building per week, taller when the week was busier, and one window per day, lit on the {{ACTIVE_DAYS}} days he contributed. A gold pennant flies over each week in which one of his pull requests was merged upstream ({{FLAG_WEEKS}} weeks, {{FLAG_PRS}} PRs). Below the city, the tools he maintains: claudemem, memory that lasts across agent sessions (about 11,000 lines of Go and nearly as many in tests); handoff, which lets a strong model delegate to cheaper ones through resumable specs, with a published evaluation; dev-orchestrator, a Claude Code plugin that takes a task to a tested pull request, failing test first. Beside them, 12 PRs merged into 10 upstream projects ({{MERGED_RANGE}}), 10 bug fixes and 2 small features: axum, SQLx and goose in Rust; AnyIO, Tenacity and Feast in Python; Zod, TanStack Query, Fastify and Claude HUD in JavaScript and TypeScript. He uses AI to find and draft fixes, verifies each one locally, and sees it through maintainer review. His production background covers multimodal evaluation and high-volume data systems; his current public focus is tools for reliable AI coding agents.</desc>
<!-- Fonts: Departure Mono (c) Helena Zhang and IBM Plex Mono (c) IBM Corp., both SIL Open Font License 1.1; embedded as renamed subsets. -->
<!-- Authored at GitHub's desktop README width: 1 unit = 1 CSS px at 846 px wide. The picture is drawn on a 3-unit
     pixel grid. Departure Mono draws an 11-pixel em, so its sizes are multiples of 11 and it lands on whole pixels.
     Plex Mono sizes give whole-unit advances (0.6 em), so lines measure the same on every platform:
     some browsers round a fractional advance to a whole pixel. text-rendering:geometricPrecision keeps
     them exact when the image is scaled (a phone or a narrow window), where Chrome otherwise rounds each
     advance to a whole screen pixel and lines grow by up to 4%. Type: 11 pixel labels, 13.33 notes
     (8-unit advance), 15 names (9), 18.33 tagline (11), 88 name.
     The caption under the tagline says what the picture is. On load, each window starts as its day's
     square in GitHub's contribution graph, a row of green squares along the horizon, and rides up as its
     building rises (dusk-scene.mjs, morph); with reduced motion the finished city shows at once.
     Under the water: a month axis and a legend; then the tools (left) and the upstream PRs (right),
     their rows on one baseline grid. -->
<defs><style>{{FONT_FACES}}
text{text-rendering:geometricPrecision}
.px{font-family:zw-pixel, ui-monospace, 'SF Mono', Menlo, Consolas, 'DejaVu Sans Mono', monospace}
.bd{font-family:zw-body, ui-monospace, 'SF Mono', Menlo, Consolas, 'DejaVu Sans Mono', monospace}
.md{font-family:zw-body-medium, ui-monospace, 'SF Mono', Menlo, Consolas, 'DejaVu Sans Mono', monospace}
.b{transform-box:fill-box;transform-origin:50% 100%;animation:rise 1.4s cubic-bezier(.65,0,.35,1) both}@keyframes rise{from{transform:scaleY(0)}to{transform:scaleY(1)}}
.m{transform-box:fill-box;transform-origin:0 0;animation-duration:1.4s;animation-timing-function:cubic-bezier(.65,0,.35,1);animation-fill-mode:both}
@keyframes mv{from{transform:var(--t)}to{transform:none}}
.c0{animation-name:mv,c0}.c1{animation-name:mv,c1}.c2{animation-name:mv,c2}.c3{animation-name:mv,c3}.c4{animation-name:mv,c4}
@keyframes c0{0%{fill:#161b22}100%{fill:#3d2c38}}@keyframes c1{0%{fill:#0e4429}100%{fill:#f6db95}}@keyframes c2{0%{fill:#006d32}100%{fill:#f6db95}}
@keyframes c3{0%{fill:#26a641}100%{fill:#f6db95}}@keyframes c4{0%{fill:#39d353}100%{fill:#f6db95}}
.tw{animation:tw 3.4s ease-in-out infinite}@keyframes tw{0%,100%{opacity:.2}50%{opacity:.95}}
.glow{animation:glow 7s ease-in-out infinite}@keyframes glow{0%,100%{opacity:.75}50%{opacity:1}}
.sh{animation:sh 2.8s ease-in-out infinite}@keyframes sh{0%,100%{opacity:.25}50%{opacity:.9}}
.beacon{animation:bk 1.6s steps(1) infinite}@keyframes bk{0%{opacity:1}50%{opacity:.15}}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){*{animation:none!important}}</style>
